FreeBSD-EN-26:30.vfs                                            Errata Notice
                                                          The FreeBSD Project

Topic:          Large file reads produce incorrect return value

Category:       core
Module:         vfs
Announced:      2026-11-17
Credits:        Thomas Waldmann <tw@waldmann-edv.de>
Affects:        All supported versions of FreeBSD.
Corrected:      2026-11-05 19:32:59 UTC (stable/15, 15.1-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:07:29 UTC (releng/15.1, 15.1-RELEASE-p7)
                2026-11-14 15:49:06 UTC (stable/14, 14.5-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:04:55 UTC (releng/14.5, 14.5-RELEASE-p4)
                2026-11-17 17:03:55 UTC (releng/14.4, 14.4-RELEASE-p13)

For general information regarding FreeBSD Errata Notices and Security
Advisories, including descriptions of the fields above, security
branches, and the following sections, please visit
<URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

The uiomove() function is used within the kernel to copy data between
buffers when either the source, the destination, or both are in user
space, particularly for the read(2) / recv(2) and write(2) / send(2)
families of system calls.  A single uiomove() call can process at most
2^31 - 1 bytes (one less than 2 GiB), less than a single read(2) may
request.

II.  Problem Description

When a process read from a file, the kernel first issued a dummy
uiomove() call to ensure that the destination buffer was mapped and
loaded into physical memory, without checking that the requested
length was within the range that uiomove() accepts.  As a consequence
of arithmetic overflow, although the read itself was performed
correctly, uiomove() miscounted the amount of memory that was mapped,
and this incorrect value was returned to the caller as the amount of
data that was received.

III. Impact

The exact impact will depend on how closely the application checks the
return value from the read operation.  In the most common case, a read(2)
call will appear to return more data than was requested, and the
application will either interpret this as an I/O error or blindly attempt
to access the additional data and crash.  Applications that never attempt
to read 2 GiB or more in a single operation are not affected.

IV.  Workaround

Set the debug.vn_io_fault_enable sysctl to 0:

# sysctl debug.vn_io_fault_enable=0

To keep this setting after a reboot, add the line
"debug.vn_io_fault_enable=0" to /etc/sysctl.conf.

V.   Solution

Upgrade your system to a supported FreeBSD stable or release / security
branch (releng) dated after the correction date, and reboot the system.

Perform one of the following:

1) To update your system installed from base system packages:

Systems running a 15.0-RELEASE or later version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or
arm64 platforms, which were installed using base system packages, can be
updated via the pkg(8) utility:

# pkg upgrade -r FreeBSD-base
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for an erratum fix"

2) To update your system installed from binary distribution sets:

Systems running a RELEASE version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or arm64 platforms
which were not installed using base system packages can be updated via the
freebsd-update(8) utility:

# freebsd-update fetch
# freebsd-update install
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for an erratum fix"

3) To update your system via a source code patch:

The following patches have been verified to apply to the applicable
FreeBSD release branches.

a) Download the relevant patch from the location below, and verify the
detached PGP signature using your PGP utility.

[FreeBSD 15.x]
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:30/vfs-15.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:30/vfs-15.patch.asc
# gpg --verify vfs-15.patch.asc

[FreeBSD 14.x]
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:30/vfs-14.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:30/vfs-14.patch.asc
# gpg --verify vfs-14.patch.asc

b) Apply the patch.  Execute the following commands as root:

# cd /usr/src
# patch -E -p0 < /path/to/patch

c) Recompile your kernel as described in
<URL:https://www.FreeBSD.org/handbook/kernelconfig.html> and reboot the
system.

VI.  Correction details

This issue is corrected as of the corresponding Git commit hash in the
following stable and release branches:

Branch/path                             Hash                     Revision
